#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093260} :activity/id #uuid "0a81722a-49ce-5840-a251-9d8336e53561" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049891 :topic/id #uuid "1bf5a1e3-35e9-581e-b44a-fa6e4c7b4bff" :topic/math-academy-id 932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093433 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf R$ by its standard matrix $R,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{R}$ by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =R(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {j}^{'} & =R(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/35/3516ba56a842adb78f1acae9190b65eb1574629e9f3d6bca25854f547f69385f.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation $\\mathbf R$ represented by the matrix $R$ defines a **counterclockwise rotation of $30^\\circ$ about the origin.**\n\nAs usual, we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation $\\mathbf R$ can also be considered a rotation of $360^\\circ-30^\\circ=330^\\circ$ *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "427eca20-6aad-5c52-a545-fc66d808e5b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3546 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b310d56-7a89-527c-bf59-b2f90684f0c7" :step/math-academy-id 464465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093261}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093435 :tutorial/content "The matrix $R_\\theta,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nrepresents a **rotation by an angle $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.**\n\nTo find the matrix representing a rotation by an angle $\\theta$ *clockwise* about the origin, we replace $\\theta$ with $-\\theta$ in the above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(-\\theta ) & -\\operatorname{sin}(-\\theta ) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(-\\theta ) & \\operatorname{cos}(-\\theta )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "333a7749-1cde-5720-8a91-4a43d26fc0fd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3548 :tutorial/title "General Rotation Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "96b175ec-ed88-5e8d-8030-67a565348dd4" :step/math-academy-id 464466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092344 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092067 :question/id #uuid "fbf1464e-93e7-5f12-92d3-d0c96a37b2be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5459"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "add52a4e-fff5-5cf6-8fe9-85a44063e2a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292889} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218629289